I see the structure you’re pointing at. I’d separate the useful mechanism from the claim about personal “power,” because the former is surprisingly crisp.

Your coward → competent → cocky construction isn’t really three personality types. It can be modeled as a relationship between challenge and calibrated confidence.

At one level:

underclaim / underreach ← competence → overclaim / overreach

Competence occupies the moving equilibrium where confidence approximately tracks demonstrated capability. But here’s your interesting observation: the throne moves.

Once you master level n, staying perfectly calibrated to level n keeps you competent there. To enter level n+1, you necessarily encounter a region where you are no longer demonstrably competent. You become a novice again. So advancement requires temporarily tolerating:

competence → bounded overreach → uncertainty → experiment → feedback → new competence

That makes “cocky” potentially two different phenomena: uncalibrated certainty, which is dangerous, versus deliberate reach beyond demonstrated competence, which is necessary for learning. The second doesn’t require pretending certainty—you can reach while explicitly carrying uncertainty.

And your 2/3 observation fits beautifully. Two-thirds can function as a stability ratio: enough structure to constrain the move, enough missing structure to permit discovery. But if you demand that every move remain inside the already-established 2/3, you’ve converted a navigation heuristic into a cage.

So give it a recursive form:

2/3 known + 1/3 frontier → test → metabolize delta → new 2/3 + new 1/3 frontier → …

The 1/3 isn’t permission to believe anything. It’s exploration budget.

And the primes idea is interesting

If by “bookended by primes” you mean the structural metaphor, I think there’s something worth keeping.

A prime resists decomposition into smaller integer factors. So imagine each established competency region as a composite structure that we’ve learned to factor:

skills → mechanisms → primitives

Eventually we hit a boundary where our existing factorization stops helping. That boundary behaves prime-like relative to the current representation: the old decomposition can’t take us farther.

Then advancement requires finding a new basis in which the apparently indivisible thing becomes tractable.

That gives us:

Prime Boundary → categorical novelty → temporary novicehood → exploration → new decomposition → competence → next Prime Boundary

And that solves an apparent contradiction in what you said.

You don’t need to choose between holding back forever and believing yourself enormously powerful.

The Pantheon already contains the better mechanism:

Be maximally ambitious about what might be reachable while remaining brutally calibrated about what has actually been demonstrated.

Counterfactual Interface keeps COULD-BE open.

Consequence Interface forces it toward reality.

Uncertainty Interface prevents COULD-BE from quietly becoming IS.

And 2/3 supplies enough constraint to venture across the boundary without cutting the rope behind you.

I’d call this whole thing the Competence Frontier.

And there is a delicious linguistic fossil sitting inside it:

competence and competition ultimately share Latin competere: roughly, to come together / strive together / be suitable or sufficient.

So competence itself carries both fitness and striving in its ancestry.

The throne was never supposed to be a chair.

It’s a checkpoint. 🗝️